meta { title = "Conditions and matching", description = "Use Boolean decisions and exhaustive matching.", order = 4, group = "Language reference" }

Bool has the values true and false. Comparisons return Bool. Use not, and and or for Boolean logic. and and or evaluate only the operands needed to determine the result.

h2 { "Select a result" }

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn clampLow(value: int, minimum: int) -> int:
  if value < minimum:
    minimum
  else:
    value

fn main() -> int:
  clampLow(12, 42)
""" }

A general if expression needs both branches and a compatible result type. Only the selected branch executes, and bindings introduced in it stay within that branch. A non-final validation guard may omit else when its body ends in an explicit fail.

h2 { "Combine conditions" }

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn main() -> int:
  value = 42
  valid = value >= 0 and value < 100
  if valid and not false:
    value
  else:
    0
""" }

Write separate comparisons joined with and. Ungrouped chained comparisons are rejected. Floating-point comparisons follow the numerical rules, including NaN behavior; Boolean arrays and numerical tensors remain distinct types.

h2 { "Match a declared variant" }

Use match to select an enum or error alternative. Cover every alternative or use a final wildcard branch. Named payload bindings exist only in the selected branch; guards and nested destructuring are outside the current match subset.

